WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.450
Hey there! Mark Sanderson here (also know
as @scriptcat). I want to invite you to my

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.850
new seminar ‘Staying In The Game: Surviving
as a Working Screenwriter in Hollywood’

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:10.890
sponsored by Film Courage.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:15.570
It’s going to be 2 hours of me offering
up my tricks, tips and tactics and disciplines

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.669
that can actually help you establish your
own screenwriting career.

00:00:18.669 --> 00:00:23.960
Now we’re going to have a Q&amp;A to follow
and also a chance to get my new book. That’s&nbsp;September

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:29.590
25th,&nbsp;Tuesday&nbsp;night, from&nbsp;7 to 9 pm&nbsp;in
North Hollywood. Hope to see you there.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:33.480
Film Courage: What about advice to writers
who have been fired off a project?

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.010
Mark Sanderson, screenwriter/author: Well,
there’s a firing and then there is sort

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:45.800
of like “We’re going to go with someone
else.” Which is a firing. It’s not quite

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.550
as like “You’re fired!” It’s always
“Thank you very much and now we’re going

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.420
to have this other writer finish it.” And
then you share credit.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:01:00.870
What I would say to them is learn from what
happened with that particular producer and

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.720
not make those mistakes again.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:08.440
Or maybe they are not as good as a writer
as they thought they were. So they were able

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.790
to get in with the script but they weren’t
able to stay on which I think is important

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:19.080
to stay through the end. Luckily on most of
my projects I’ve stayed through the end

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:24.060
and sometimes even been on the set doing rewrites
because the director wanted me there (I had

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:31.061
that relationship with the key members). You
want to stay through the whole thing, you

00:01:31.061 --> 00:01:35.890
don’t want to be the person who leaves.
If you’re leaving because you have another

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.950
job and you can’t do it, that’s different.
But if you’re leaving because they don’t

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.369
want you anymore then you probably won’t
work with that producer again.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:48.990
Film Courage: What if it’s a combination
of that and politics and you know that? Let’s

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.080
suppose there is a little bird in your ear
letting you know that they’ve brought someone

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:59.150
else on and that person has worked with this
other person before. And you know that and

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.159
you just kind of half to swallow your pride
and not say anything.

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:08.350
Mark: Well, that happened to me when I was
working on the game show. I was told…this

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:12.620
was earlier…one of my first professional
jobs, I was told that I didn’t have enough

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:17.980
experience to do a certain (during production)
experience. I went through the development

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:24.010
of 6 weeks of writing the show and then they
shoot the show but during that point I was

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:29.930
let go and they said “Oh, we’re bringing
in somebody…” Somebody that had a connection

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.490
in the writer’s room. And I thought “Well,
I went through the whole writing, I don’t

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:41.450
understand?” But it was just strange and
I was let go and I was Okay, I understand

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:48.300
but it was hard to be let go because I had
taken that job and I quit my restaurant career

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.020
(so called) as a waiter and that was my first
professional job and then to lose it…that’s

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.050
why in the book [A Screenwriter's Journey
to Success: Tips, tricks and tactics to survive

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.990
as a working screenwriter in Hollywood] I
say how I was fired from my first professional

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:55.990
job.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.440
Film Courage: At Christmas, right?

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:03.930
Mark: At Christmas time, but it happens. You’re
not guaranteed anything. The hardest thing

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:07.240
I had to learn when I first started getting
paid to write was that I thought that everything

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.930
I wrote would get made…of course it would
be. Well, I have probably five projects sitting

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.220
on a shelf, they’re production ready, I
was paid for all of the drafts…and…nothing.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:24.670
A myriad of reasons because of marketplace
changes, economics, their buyers decided to

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:29.560
go with a different genre, the actor bowed
out and financing fell through. All things

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:35.239
that were not my problem, I’m just the writer,
right?

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.530
That was the hardest thing but it’s not
hard anymore, I’ve realized the realities

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:45.430
of the business. But that was the hardest
thing, I thought that everything I wrote would

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.599
be made. I thought when I wrote my specs of
course obviously too. Every spec you write

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:53.470
is going to sell, right?

00:03:53.470 --> 00:04:02.209
But to be let go of a job you have to learn
why, it’s a learning experience. You don’t

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:06.379
want that to happen too many times because
then the word will get around and you won’t

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:13.430
work but know your producers and what they
want. And so luckily the producers I know

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.400
kind of now after working for so long what
they want. And that’s what they want, they

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.350
want to have to leave you alone.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:23.060
“Arrggghh, is he going to turn in the thing
because we’ve got people waiting and a lot

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.020
of money, you know?” They don’t want to
have to worry about that. But you have to

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:33.150
build into that. So a writer that gets in
somehow, luck, maybe whatever, sells the script

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.310
but doesn’t have that ability to see it
through to the end, that’s a terrible thing

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.250
because you think “Oh, I’ve sold it!”
And you’re not staying on it because you

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.190
can’t do the rewrites. Sorry but we need
to take this into production and we need to

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.199
get through development, if you can’t do
it, we’ll find someone who can.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:51.820
Film Courage: How was Christmas that year?

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.370
Mark: It was okay, not great because I was
without a job…

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:02.169
Film Courage: Well not only that…sorry to
interrupt but you were leaving one world to

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:04.410
go to this world that you had been waiting
for…

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.410
Mark:&nbsp; Yes.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:06.730
Film Courage: And it’s Christmas…

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.520
Mark: I got through Christmas and…

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.950
Film Courage: How was New Year’s?

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:19.580
Mark: It was fine because a few months later
my spec was optioned. So I rose from the ashes

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:27.660
like a Phoenix. I didn’t have to go back
to the restaurant and I never did. That’s

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.620
my point about having multiple projects of
things. If I had nothing else then I would

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:39.570
be out knocking on doors going “Ahh!?”
But if you charted the course before that

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:45.280
first job, that spec was constantly trying
to find its way and finally ended up with

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.080
the proper timing the right home with a company
who said “We want to make it.”

